Minor domestic cat scratch presenting with localized mild erythema and transient sting
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Linear epidermal laceration, Immediate transient stinging, Narrow perilesional erythema

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-028
Reason for Visit: Acute localized pain and skin disruption following animal trauma requiring assessment to rule out deep soft tissue injury or acute zoonotic infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute animal scratch with resulting skin breakdown and pain. Urgent physical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to evaluate the depth of the laceration, assess for joint/tendon involvement, and implement immediate wound decontamination to mitigate the high risk of Pasteurella multocida or staphylococcal infection.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My cat scratched me deeply and the cut is stinging and red.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a superficial linear epidermal laceration matching a feline claw profile, sustained 2 hours ago. Reports immediate sharp localized stinging pain upon injury. Notes a narrow rim of mild erythema confined directly to the scratch margin. Minimal to zero capillary bleeding, which quickly achieved hemostasis. Complete resolution of localized pain is expected within 24 hours. Absence of systemic fever, generalized malaise, or spreading redness.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Pet ownership hygiene practices.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, focused on the localized wound.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals a superficial linear epidermal laceration with a narrow rim of mild perilesional erythema. Rapid formation of a thin protective linear crust is visible. Diagnostic triad confirmed: linear epidermal laceration, history of transient stinging, and narrow perilesional erythema. Lack of surrounding fluctuance or dermal induration. Normal regional lymph nodes without axillary or epitrochlear swelling. No evidence of deep tendon or joint capsule involvement.
Environmental Observations: Domestic cat present in the household.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Tetanus immunization status is up to date.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Minor domestic cat scratch presenting with localized mild erythema and transient sting - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Deep Tissue/Tendon Laceration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Visual confirmation of strictly superficial epidermal involvement and normal full range of motion against resistance.

Cat Scratch Disease (Bartonellosis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute presentation (hours, not weeks), absence of characteristic papule progression, and no regional lymphadenopathy.

Acute Cellulitis (Pasteurella multocida) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Extremely narrow margin of erythema, lack of rapid spread, and absence of severe throbbing pain or purulence.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient sustained a penetrating injury from a domestic feline claw, an injury with a notoriously high rate of rapidly progressing secondary infection if deep spaces are inoculated. Evaluating the patient urgently allowed for direct visualization to confirm the injury was superficial and did not breach the tendon sheath or joint capsule. Immediate physical examination safely ruled out the need for prophylactic systemic antibiotics or surgical washout, safely managing the wound conservatively.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Thorough irrigation of the wound with normal saline and chlorhexidine. Application of a sterile non-adherent dressing. Advice on monitoring for signs of infection.
Positive Behavioral Change: Cleanse any future animal scratches immediately with copious running tap water and soap for 5 full minutes, physically flushing out potential bacterial inoculums before they can replicate.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Basic wound care supplies.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the area becomes increasingly red, hot, or painful.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if red streaks develop extending from the scratch towards the heart, or if accompanied by high fever, confusion, or lethargy.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a superficial animal-inflicted mechanical wound; vigilance for delayed infection is required.
Clinical Course: The acute stinging should subside rapidly. The scratch will scab and heal over 5-7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the redness spreads rapidly, if pus begins to drain, or if you develop swollen, tender lymph nodes in your armpit or elbow.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Cat claws harbor bacteria. Because the scratch is shallow, washing it thoroughly removes the risk. The redness right at the edge is just a normal healing response, not an infection.
Actionable Steps: Keep the wound clean and dry. Watch the red border closely; trace it with a pen if you are unsure if it is expanding.

